sections.body:
An `if` statement evaluates a condition's truthiness (see Data Types for what counts as truthy or falsy) and runs its body only when the condition is true. `elif` ('else if') chains further conditions, and `else` catches whatever no earlier branch matched.

Python checks the conditions **top to bottom and stops at the first one that's true**. Comparisons can be chained, so `elif 70 <= score < 80:` reads like the math, and a one-line conditional expression, `grade = "pass" if score >= 70 else "fail"`, covers the simple two-way case. At most one branch of an if/elif/.../else chain runs, even if several conditions would be true. Several independent `if` statements in a row behave differently: each one is checked on its own.



```python
score = 85

# elif chain: only ONE branch runs
if score >= 90:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 80:
    grade = "B"      # this one runs; score >= 70 is never even checked
elif score >= 70:
    grade = "C"
else:
    grade = "F"

# separate ifs: EVERY condition is checked independently
if score >= 90:
    print("Excellent")
if score >= 80:
    print("Good")     # this prints
if score >= 70:
    print("Passing")  # this ALSO prints, even though grade was already decided as B above
```

Separate `if` statements where you meant an elif chain are a common source of bugs where several branches run together. The reverse mistake exists too: when the order of an elif chain matters, put the most specific condition first (the FizzBuzz example below shows why).

examples.code:
for i in range(1, 16):
    if i % 15 == 0:
        print("FizzBuzz")
    elif i % 3 == 0:
        print("Fizz")
    elif i % 5 == 0:
        print("Buzz")
    else:
        print(i)

